  "readme": [
    {
      "section": "dsh-workflow-canvas 🧭",
      "text": "Visual workflow builder for [DeepSeek Harness](https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness) (DSH).\nDrag and drop nodes to orchestrate **tool calls, sub-agents, and conditional branches** into a workflow that runs through DSH's `ctx.workflows` engine. Powered by [React Flow](https://github.com/xyflow/xyflow) (xyflow) and Zustand."
    },
    {
      "section": "Install",
      "text": "```sh\ndsh plugin add @piedpiper911/dsh-workflow-canvas\n```"
    },
    {
      "section": "Node types",
      "text": "| Node | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| ▶ Trigger | Workflow entry point (a prompt). |\n| ⚙ Tool | A single tool call (e.g. `video_frames`, `bash`). |\n| 🤖 Agent | Delegate a step to a sub-agent with its own prompt. |\n| ◇ Condition | Branch on an expression (then/else edges). |\n| ✓ Output | Terminal result template. |"
    },
    {
      "section": "How it works",
      "text": "1. Build a graph on the canvas: drag nodes from the toolbar, connect them with edges.\n2. The store compiles the graph into an **ordered step list** (breadth-first from triggers, conditions become branches) — this is the exact shape DSH's workflow engine consumes.\n3. `Run` hands the steps to `ctx.workflows`; `Export` saves a `.workflow.json` you can reload later."
    },
    {
      "section": "How it works",
      "text": "The data model is deliberately transport-agnostic: the canvas is a *view* over a plain-JSON workflow spec, so it composes with any DSH workflow provider."
    },
    {
      "section": "Development",
      "text": "```sh\nnpm ci\nnpm run dev        # standalone demo at :5173\nnpm run build      # tsc + vite build\n```"
    },
    {
      "section": "License",
      "text": "MIT"
    }
  ],
